WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.130
Film Courage: When you’re reading screenplays
from unknown writers, how far into the screenplay

00:00:04.130 --> 00:00:05.820
do you know whether it’s good or bad?

00:00:05.820 --> 00:00:08.210
Naomi Beaty, Writer, Screenwriting Teacher
and Consultant: I think problems can show

00:00:08.210 --> 00:00:16.720
up very easy in a script, but they don’t
always. Some scripts have a great set-up,

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:21.240
they have a great first act and they have
the right pace right away, they get the story

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:27.990
rolling, and we feel like things are happening,
we’re invested and then in Act 2 things

00:00:27.990 --> 00:00:31.699
fall apart basically like if their protagonist
stops pursuing the goal, we don’t really

00:00:31.699 --> 00:00:39.710
know what’s going on. I guess the answer
is if a script has global problems that sort

00:00:39.710 --> 00:00:47.730
of cover the entire script then you will problem
know within the first five pages or so. Definitely

00:00:47.730 --> 00:00:54.309
I think if it has major structural problems
with all the structure, overall structure

00:00:54.309 --> 00:01:00.360
as a whole, I would say by page 15 or so you
know that there’s a problem because you’re

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:04.359
waiting for something to happen and one of
the most common problems is that nothing is

00:01:04.359 --> 00:01:10.070
happening, like it’s just not getting going.
I would say you can know right away…you

00:01:10.070 --> 00:01:14.770
can know pretty quickly that a script has
some issues or needs some work. But I would

00:01:14.770 --> 00:01:19.329
say you can’t always tell what a script
or often you can’t tell what a script’s

00:01:19.329 --> 00:01:24.450
issues are until later, until you’ve seen
sort of what they are trying to accomplish

00:01:24.450 --> 00:01:28.619
or gotten a true sense of like what the story
really is or what they’re going for and

00:01:28.619 --> 00:01:32.810
a lot of times you don’t have that sense
of what they’re going for until later in

00:01:32.810 --> 00:01:35.680
the script, until towards the end of Act 2
at least.

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:42.180
Film Courage: So that probably sheds light
on the need for really having your script

00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:47.689
in good shape before you submit it and it’s
not your personal thing it’s more of an

00:01:47.689 --> 00:01:52.299
industry standard that if something within
the first few pages isn’t good it’s just

00:01:52.299 --> 00:01:54.079
going to be rejected for time’s sake.

00:01:54.079 --> 00:01:58.770
Naomi: Yes, I will say usually when I’m
reading a script I’m reading it to help.

00:01:58.770 --> 00:02:03.439
I’m reading it to try and figure out what
isn’t working and see where I can provide

00:02:03.439 --> 00:02:08.459
that insight to the writer so that they can
make it better. A lot of people in the industry

00:02:08.459 --> 00:02:12.590
as you know are not reading for that purpose,
they’re looking for projects that they want

00:02:12.590 --> 00:02:17.890
to get involved in and so since you’re submitting
your script you are sort of asking someone

00:02:17.890 --> 00:02:23.270
to get involved in your project. Hopefully
what you’ve submitted will get them excited

00:02:23.270 --> 00:02:29.489
and interested right away so you want to be
able to get them interested on Page 1, get

00:02:29.489 --> 00:02:36.830
them sort of hooked in the story by that inciting
incident, build and escalate your story sort

00:02:36.830 --> 00:02:42.090
of throughout the rest as the character’s
pursuing that goal so that your reader is

00:02:42.090 --> 00:02:45.802
invested and emotionally engaged and all that
stuff through the whole thing and then you

00:02:45.802 --> 00:02:51.650
want to deliver like some emotionally satisfying
resolution and hopefully some genuine emotion

00:02:51.650 --> 00:02:56.790
by the end of the story as well so that they
(the reader) comes away from your script moved

00:02:56.790 --> 00:03:02.390
and/or entertained in some way that they want
to get involved and help you make it.

00:03:02.390 --> 00:03:05.950
Film Courage: If we look back at let’s say
the recording industry they would say put

00:03:05.950 --> 00:03:13.129
the best three songs upfront first because
some A &amp; R person (I don’t really know too

00:03:13.129 --> 00:03:16.760
much about the music industry) but I just
remember hearing they are going to be listening

00:03:16.760 --> 00:03:22.569
to those first three songs and if by maybe
one or two in it’s not good, it goes in

00:03:22.569 --> 00:03:23.749
a bad pile.

00:03:23.749 --> 00:03:31.459
Naomi: Although the sort of flip side of doing
that I guess there’s a lot of emphasis placed

00:03:31.459 --> 00:03:36.269
on making sure that your first 10 pages is
so good and really hooky and grabs your reader,

00:03:36.269 --> 00:03:41.209
which is great. Of course you want to do that
but I have ready scripts where it was very

00:03:41.209 --> 00:03:47.700
clear that the first 10 pages were really
worked on and then after that there were clear

00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:52.819
issues that still hadn’t been addressed
and so you don’t want that either. You want

00:03:52.819 --> 00:03:57.639
a great strong start that hooks your reader
but then you want to deliver on what you’ve

00:03:57.639 --> 00:04:04.739
promised them. You want them to become emotionally
invested and to stay engaged all the way through

00:04:04.739 --> 00:04:09.010
and to really care about the outcome and to
feel something at the end.

00:04:09.010 --> 00:04:13.930
Film Courage: Interesting, so then you can’t
really cheat it where you’re like I’m

00:04:13.930 --> 00:04:18.690
going to make these 10 pages the top three
songs of the album and then it turns out that

00:04:18.690 --> 00:04:23.479
the rest of the script is all just B-side
stuff that no one wants to listen to and that’s

00:04:23.479 --> 00:04:25.530
going to bite you in the end too?

00:04:25.530 --> 00:04:28.930
Naomi: Yes, I don’t think you can do that
because the purpose of making your first 10

00:04:28.930 --> 00:04:33.759
pages really, really strong is to get someone
to read the rest of it, right? So the rest

00:04:33.759 --> 00:04:34.759
of it has to be worth reading, too.

00:04:34.759 --> 00:04:34.775
Question For The Viewers: How many pages does
it take you to detect a bad screenplay?

